#
# WHY THIS IS AN ALLOWLIST AND NOT A LIST OF EXCLUSIONS
#
# The build context is what the Dockerfile's first stage copies in, and that
# stage is a real image layer whether or not anybody pushes it. An exclusion
# list has to name every private path, which means a directory added next month
# is included by default and somebody has to remember. This repository has a
# public remote, an untracked `private/` tree, an untracked `DEFERRED.md` naming
# internal hosts, and a `.claude/` directory, so "included by default" is the
# wrong default.
#
# So everything is excluded and the build's inputs are named back in. A path
# that is not listed here cannot reach a layer by accident, and adding an input
# is a visible line in a diff.
#
# It also keeps the context small: unfiltered it is 268 MB before `target/`,
# which is 2.6 GB on its own.

*

# The workspace, its lockfile and the pinned toolchain.
!Cargo.toml
!Cargo.lock
!rust-toolchain.toml
!crates

# Compiled into the binary by crates/stegobench-cli/build.rs, so they are build
# inputs rather than runtime files. Leaving any of them out produces a binary
# that starts, runs, and has lost half of what it needs: no registry, no
# self-test fixtures, no adapters, no starter corpus.
!plugins/registry
!plugins/adapters
!fixtures
!corpora/starter

# Shipped in the final image beside the binary, because a container with no
# licence text in it is a container somebody has to go and look up.
!README.md
!LICENSE
!CHANGELOG.md

# Re-excluded under the directories admitted above. `target` is the local build
# tree, and a stale one in the context would be copied in and then ignored;
# `__pycache__` and the starter's builder are not inputs to a Rust build.
crates/**/target
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
